ARC = [
{"es": "The Fool", "kw": "freedom, impulse, journey", "sombra": "flight, scattering, denying every limit",
 "mirar": "He is the only one who walks. He moves in the direction of reading, his bundle over his shoulder: he carries everything he is. An animal pushes him or bites his leg, the instinct that will not let him stand still. His staff touches the ground: he moves without quite losing the earth. He has no number because he holds no place in the series: he crosses all of it.",
 "amp": "He descends from the court jester, the only one allowed to tell the king the truth, and survives as the Joker of the modern deck. He is kin to Parsifal, who finds the Grail because he does not know he cannot, to the holy fool of mystical traditions and to the trickster of myth. In Jungian terms he is psychic energy before it takes shape: the beginning and the end of the journey of individuation.",
 "q": ["Where would you walk if nobody were watching?", "What are you carrying in your bundle that you no longer need?", "What is biting your leg and forcing you to move?"],
 "hs": [["The bundle", "Everything he is fits in a bag over his shoulder. Luggage or memory?"],
        ["The animal", "In the Marseille deck it bites his leg; here it walks ahead of him. Instinct pushes or opens the way: there is no journey without it."],
        ["The gaze towards the light", "He looks up, not at the ground he treads: he walks on the edge of the abyss, guided by what he cannot yet reach."],
        ["The staff", "It touches the rock: he moves without quite losing the earth."]]},
{"es": "The Magician", "kw": "beginning, choosing, skill", "sombra": "trickery, scattering, manipulation",
 "mirar": "There is a bit of everything on the table: cups, dice, a knife, coins, a pouch. They are the four suits in miniature, and he has not yet decided what to use. One hand holds the wand, the other a coin: he joins what is above and what is below. The table shows three legs; the fourth falls outside the card, as if the gesture were completed in the space of whoever is looking.",
 "amp": "A bateleur is a fairground conjurer, not the ceremonial magician invented by nineteenth-century occultism, and that ambiguity is his richness. Hermes belongs to him, god of beginnings, roads and thieves, and so does the alchemical Mercury that sets the work in motion. Nichols calls him creator and trickster: the consciousness that begins to order the world and can be fooled by its own tricks.",
 "q": ["What do you already have on the table that you are not using?", "If you had to start with a single object, which would it be?", "Where does your talent end and your trick begin?"],
 "hs": [["The hat", "Wide, wavy brim. In the Marseille deck it draws a figure eight lying down, infinity as the horizon of whoever begins. Strength wears the same one."],
        ["The table", "Cups, dice, coins: the four suits in miniature, still unchosen."],
        ["The raised wand", "One hand up, one hand down: the Magician is a channel between planes."],
        ["The light on the table", "A beam falls exactly where he works: what begins receives something it does not make."]]},
{"es": "The Popess", "kw": "gestation, inner knowledge, silence", "sombra": "coldness, closure, judgement",
 "mirar": "She sits still, wrapped in layers: veil, mantle, robe. She is not reading the open book on her lap: she is incubating it. Behind her, a curtain separates what is shown from what is kept. This is degree two: accumulating without acting yet, preparing in silence what is to come.",
 "amp": "The legend of Pope Joan, the scandal of a woman on Peter's throne, meets the priestess of the mysteries, veiled Isis and the Sophia of the Gnostics. Nichols reads her as the woman who follows her vocation even when it leaves her alone; Mary Greer suggests seeing her today as introversion that trusts intuition. For Jung she would be the wise anima, the door to the unconscious.",
 "q": ["What are you incubating that should not come out yet?", "Which of your books is written but still unread?", "Does your silence protect you or lock you in?"],
 "hs": [["The open book", "She does not read it: she gestates it. Knowledge not yet spoken."],
        ["The egg", "An egg glows beside her: what is incubated until it is ready to be born."],
        ["The hanging veil", "A cloth hangs above her: it separates what is shown from what is kept."],
        ["The tiara", "Three crowns stacked up: a knowledge that touches what exceeds her."]]},
{"es": "The Empress", "kw": "creation, fertility, expression", "sombra": "excess, vanity, scattering",
 "mirar": "She is the first figure to look straight at us. After the stillness of the Popess, here everything sprouts: wings behind the throne, a flowering sceptre, a shield with an eagle that has not yet taken flight. This is degree three: accumulated energy bursts into form, without yet knowing where it is going.",
 "amp": "Demeter, who makes the fields grow; Venus, who engenders; the Great Mother whom Nichols calls queen of heaven and earth. Her reverse is the mother who holds on, because the same archetype nourishes and devours. In medieval art, the Virgin in majesty holds the world as she holds the shield: what is created as a child.",
 "q": ["What wants to be born through you?", "Where does your abundance turn into excess?", "What do you create when you are not looking for results?"],
 "hs": [["The shield", "In the Marseille deck it bears a folded eagle: creation that has not yet decided its flight. Compare it with the Emperor's."],
        ["The wings", "They frame the throne: what creates is not entirely on earth."],
        ["The sceptre", "Resting, not gripped: power that blossoms instead of imposing."],
        ["The raised face", "She looks upwards: creating is also receiving."]]},
{"es": "The Emperor", "kw": "stability, order, mastery", "sombra": "rigidity, control, authoritarianism",
 "mirar": "He leans on his throne rather than sitting on it: he has the confidence of someone with nothing to prove. His crossed legs draw a four, the number of stability. The eagle on his shield lies on the ground and already has a shape: the Empress's creation turned into law.",
 "amp": "Zeus, king of the Olympian order, and the Roman pater familias; for Nichols, the father of civilisation who founds cities and laws. His shadow is the tyrant and the father who does not let others grow. Jung describes the father archetype as a structure that gives form to the chaos of the psyche: indispensable and, when made absolute, suffocating.",
 "q": ["Which structure holds you up, and which one locks you in?", "Where do you need to set a clear limit?", "What authority have you inherited without questioning it?"],
 "hs": [["The crossed legs", "In the traditional card they form the figure 4: the square, the earth, what does not move."],
        ["The shield on the ground", "It rests beside the throne: power that no longer needs defending."],
        ["The sceptre and orb", "He governs the material world: the orb is the earth in his hand."],
        ["In profile", "He does not look at us: he looks at what he rules. Order cares for the world, not for itself."]]},
{"es": "The Pope", "kw": "mediation, teaching, ideal", "sombra": "dogma, hypocrisy, dependence",
 "mirar": "He is the first to have someone to speak to: two small disciples, seen from behind in the foreground, are listening. Between two columns, he blesses with one hand and holds a triple cross with the other. This is degree five: an ideal appears, and someone acts as a bridge towards it.",
 "amp": "Pontiff literally means bridge-builder. With him stand the hierophant of Eleusis, the Zen master, the analyst who accompanies: every mediator between the human and what exceeds it. Nichols calls him the visible face of God; his shadow is the institution that replaces experience with obedience.",
 "q": ["Who teaches you today, and whom do you teach?", "Which belief do you repeat without having lived it?", "Towards which ideal are you building a bridge?"],
 "hs": [["The two disciples", "We see them from behind: we are one more in the group that listens."],
        ["The blessing hand", "He passes on, he does not possess: knowledge circulates."],
        ["The triple cross", "Three crossbars: body, soul and spirit, or three worlds joined."],
        ["The vertical light", "It descends upon him: he is a bridge between what is above and those who listen."]]},
{"es": "The Lover", "kw": "choice, union, desire", "sombra": "indecision, dependence, confusion",
 "mirar": "Three people and an angel. The young man stands between two women who touch him or claim him; his feet point in different directions. Above, Cupid aims his arrow from inside a sun. This is degree six: discovering the other, doing what one loves. Its name, LAMOVREVX, is one of the two that did not change over the centuries.",
 "amp": "The scene has been read as the choice between vice and virtue, like Hercules at the crossroads, or between the mother and the beloved. Nichols speaks of Cupid's golden error: love chooses us before we choose. In Jungian terms, it is the encounter with the anima or animus that pulls the ego out of its centre.",
 "q": ["Which two voices are you caught between right now?", "What do you do only because it gives you pleasure?", "Which choice are you letting others make for you?"],
 "hs": [["Cupid in the sun", "The arrow comes from above: desire is not decided, it is received."],
        ["The hands", "Each woman approaches the young man in a different way. Every bond has its gesture."],
        ["Seen from behind", "We see them from the back, as if we stood among them: the choice is ours too."],
        ["The flowers", "One of them offers flowers: pleasure as a gift."]]},
{"es": "The Chariot", "kw": "action, progress, will", "sombra": "empty conquest, arrogance, running others over",
 "mirar": "A crowned prince stands in a square chariot under a canopy. Two horses pull, one blue and one red, looking in different directions. He holds no reins. This is degree seven: action in the world, which moves forward not through control but through agreement between opposing forces.",
 "amp": "In Plato's Phaedrus, the charioteer drives two horses, one noble and one unruly: an image of the soul. The triumphal carts of the Renaissance paraded laden with allegories, the likely origin of the tarot's trumps, and they survive in merry-go-rounds. Nichols says the Chariot takes us home: the outer conquest that is also a return.",
 "q": ["Which two forces pull you in different directions?", "Where are you heading without reins?", "What victory are you after, and what for?"],
 "hs": [["Two horses", "One blue, one red, no reins, each pulling its own way. Progress is born of agreement, not of the whip."],
        ["The moons on the shoulders", "Two crescents: what looks back and what looks ahead."],
        ["The crown", "A young prince who already reigns: action with direction."],
        ["The open sky", "He moves beneath an opening of light: he acts under something greater than himself."]]},
{"es": "Justice", "kw": "balance, discernment, truth", "sombra": "rigidity, merciless judgement, perfectionism",
 "mirar": "Frontal, between columns, with an upright sword and a pair of scales. This is degree eight: receptive perfection, nothing too much and nothing missing. Unlike the modern allegory, she wears no blindfold: she sees. A foot peeks out from under her robe, as if she were about to stand up.",
 "amp": "Maat, the Egyptian feather that weighs the hearts of the dead; Themis and Astraea, the justice that left the earth because of humankind. Nichols asks whether justice exists: human justice is imperfect, and the card speaks rather of an inner balance that has to be sustained every day.",
 "q": ["What are you weighing, and by what standard?", "Where are you harder on yourself than on others?", "What would you have to cut to recover your balance?"],
 "hs": [["The scales", "In balance, not at rest: balancing is a continuous act."],
        ["The upright sword", "It cuts away what is superfluous. To discern is to separate."],
        ["No blindfold", "The Justice of the tarot looks. Judging without seeing would be something else."],
        ["The foot that shows", "A foot emerges from beneath the mantle: Justice is not still, she is about to move."]]},
{"es": "The Hermit", "kw": "crisis, search, wisdom", "sombra": "isolation, bitterness, flight from the world",
 "mirar": "He walks to the left, backwards in the series, towards the past. He raises a lantern half-hidden by his own mantle: his light is for those who come after him. This is degree nine: the fertile crisis of the end, withdrawing so that something new can begin.",
 "amp": "Diogenes with his lamp, looking for an honest man; Cronus and Saturn, the old time that ripens things; the desert fathers. Nichols asks whether anybody is there: the loneliness of the seeker. Jung would link him to the archetype of the wise old man, the inner guide who appears when the ego no longer knows.",
 "q": ["What do you need to leave behind in order to see?", "For whom do you carry your light?", "Is your retreat a search or a refuge?"],
 "hs": [["The lantern", "He holds it up in front of him: it lights only the next step."],
        ["The mantle", "Layers of experience that weigh down and shelter at the same time."],
        ["The staff", "It touches the earth: wisdom that does not lose the ground."],
        ["The distant light", "A small star in the night: his lantern answers another light."]]},
{"es": "The Wheel of Fortune", "kw": "cycle, change, opportunity", "sombra": "fatalism, instability, passivity",
 "mirar": "A wheel with a crank and nobody to turn it. One animal climbs, another falls, and at the top a crowned sphinx stays motionless with its sword. This is degree ten: the end of one cycle and the announcement of the next. The question lies in the crank: who turns it?",
 "amp": "Boethius's Fortune, who in The Consolation of Philosophy turns her wheel indifferent to kings and beggars; the wheel of samsara; the chariot of time. Nichols gives her chapter a cry for help: the ego discovers it does not govern the cycles. The sphinx suggests another way out: not fleeing the turning, but standing at its centre.",
 "q": ["Where are you on the wheel: rising, falling or at the top?", "Which cycle is closing in your life?", "If the crank were yours, would you turn it?"],
 "hs": [["The crank", "The wheel does not turn by itself: a hand is missing. Perhaps yours."],
        ["The winged figure", "Crowned above the wheel, still while everything turns: the motionless centre of the cycle."],
        ["Those who rise and fall", "Some climb, others fall: no place on the wheel is permanent."],
        ["The axis of light", "The centre shines: whatever turns has a point that does not move."]]},
{"es": "Strength", "kw": "deep energy, new beginning, integration", "sombra": "repression, violence, domination of instinct",
 "mirar": "She opens the lion's mouth with no apparent effort, and we cannot tell whether she is opening it or closing it. She wears the same lemniscate hat as the Magician: this is the second beginning, that of the second series. The Magician begins with skill; Strength begins from the depths, from the animal.",
 "amp": "Hercules against the Nemean lion, but without a club: the strength that does not kill instinct but integrates it. Daniel in the lions' den, the maiden who tames the unicorn. Nichols asks: whose strength? In Jungian terms, the relationship between the ego and instinctual energy which, when repressed, turns against us.",
 "q": ["Which of your instincts needs to be heard rather than tamed?", "Where do you use force when calm would be enough?", "What begins in you from below, and not from the head?"],
 "hs": [["Hands in the jaws", "Neither fight nor flight: she gently opens or closes the beast's mouth. Integrating is not defeating."],
        ["The lion", "Huge and yet docile: instinct that has been accepted is not frightening."],
        ["The lemniscate hat", "The same figure eight lying down that the Magician wears: two beginnings, two series. Here it crowns her head like an endless thought."],
        ["The distant gaze", "She holds the beast without looking at it: the strength comes from elsewhere."]]},
{"es": "The Hanged Man", "kw": "pause, surrender, another perspective", "sombra": "paralysis, victimhood, barren sacrifice",
 "mirar": "He hangs by one foot, the other leg crossed behind and his hands hidden at his back. Nobody seems to be forcing him: his face is serene. The trees have had their branches cut, they will grow outwards no more. This is the second gestation: stopping completely and seeing the world upside down.",
 "amp": "Odin hung for nine nights from the world tree to obtain the runes. The defamatory paintings of Renaissance Italy hung traitors and debtors in effigy, upside down. Nichols titles her chapter intrigue: a card that disconcerts. Jung would see the voluntary suspension of the ego, the sacrifice that lets the unconscious speak.",
 "q": ["What do you see if you turn your problem upside down?", "What are you surrendering to, and what are you resigning yourself to?", "What do you hope will happen if you stop doing?"],
 "hs": [["Tied by one foot", "A single point of attachment: just enough not to fall, just enough to let go of everything else."],
        ["The hidden hands", "He does not act. What he has to do is not do."],
        ["The living tree", "He hangs from a leafy tree: what holds him keeps growing."],
        ["Head down", "He sees the world inverted: sometimes you have to lose a point of view to find another."]]},
{"es": "The Nameless Arcanum", "kw": "transformation, ending, cleansing", "sombra": "destruction, fear of change, clinging",
 "mirar": "It is the only card without a name. A flesh-coloured skeleton reaps dark earth from which hands, feet and heads sprout, one of them crowned. There are no corpses: there are shoots. This is the outburst of the second series: what is cut makes room.",
 "amp": "The medieval danse macabre, in which death makes king and peasant equal; Cronus with his sickle; the alchemical nigredo, the putrefaction that precedes the stone. Nichols calls it the enemy, because that is how the ego lives it. Tradition leaves it unnamed, as if what truly transforms could not be spoken.",
 "q": ["What has already ended, even if you do not accept it?", "What is sprouting in the ground you have just cleared?", "Why do you think this card has no name?"],
 "hs": [["The nameless space", "Where the other cards carry their name, here there is a gap. The unnameable is also information."],
        ["The figure", "It is not a dead man: it is the structure that remains when the superfluous falls away."],
        ["What sprouts", "Hands and heads emerge from the earth: the cut prepares new growth."],
        ["The scythe", "It reaps in a curve: it cuts so that something can grow again."]]},
{"es": "Temperance", "kw": "circulation, care, living balance", "sombra": "lukewarmness, stagnation, avoiding conflict",
 "mirar": "An angel pours a liquid from one jar into another, and the stream seems to defy gravity. It is the first winged being after Arcanum XIII: after the reaping, something circulates. This is degree four of the second series: stability is no longer stillness, it is flow.",
 "amp": "To temper is to mix in the right measure: wine with water, hot metal with cold water. Iris, messenger of the rainbow; the guardian angel. Nichols calls her celestial alchemy, the union of opposites that Jung considered the heart of the psychic work.",
 "q": ["What in your life needs to circulate instead of piling up?", "Which two parts of you could you mix?", "What do you take care of without anyone seeing?"],
 "hs": [["The two jars", "The water passes from one to the other: a physics that is not ours."],
        ["The wings", "The first winged figure after XIII: the protection that follows the cut."],
        ["Flowers in her hair", "A thought that blossoms."],
        ["Water reaching the ground", "The stream overflows and reaches the earth: the mixing ends in the concrete."]]},
{"es": "The Devil", "kw": "desire, energy, creative shadow", "sombra": "dependence, addiction, deceit",
 "mirar": "A figure with horns, bat wings and attributes of both sexes, standing on a pedestal. Below, two little devils tied by the neck, though the rope is slack: they could free themselves. This is degree five of the second series: mediation, but from below.",
 "amp": "Pan, the goat god of wild nature; Lucifer, the light-bearer; Baphomet, an invention of nineteenth-century occultism. It is the only card Jung spoke about specifically: he saw in it a hermaphroditic figure, perhaps the gold of alchemy, the union of opposites that the Christian mindset fears. Nichols calls him the dark angel: the shadow that holds creative energy.",
 "q": ["What ties you down that you could actually let go of?", "Which desire do you hide because its strength frightens you?", "What energy in your shadow could become creative?"],
 "hs": [["The bound ones", "Two little devils joined by slack ropes: they could free themselves. Dependence is also an agreement."],
        ["The torch", "He carries light: Lucifer means light-bearer."],
        ["The double body", "Wings, horns and a human body: Jung read it as a union of opposites."],
        ["The blue flame", "In his other hand burns a fire of another colour: desire that can also become light."]]},
{"es": "The Tower", "kw": "opening, liberation, irruption", "sombra": "collapse, chaos, wounded pride",
 "mirar": "Its name is not the tower but the House of God. A flame or a feather comes down from the sky and lifts the tower's crown like a lid. Two figures fall, or come out, with their hands towards the earth. Coloured spheres rain down. This is the pleasure of six in the second series: what was locked in finally opens.",
 "amp": "Babel, the tower of pride scattered into languages; Zeus's thunderbolt; the fairy-tale castle that crumbles when the spell is broken. Nichols calls it the liberating blow: what looks like catastrophe knocks down a structure that had already become a prison. Jung speaks of the irruption of the unconscious when the ego has shut itself in too tightly.",
 "q": ["Which structure in your life had already become a prison?", "What would come out if the lid opened?", "Are they falling, or coming out?"],
 "hs": [["The feather of fire", "What bursts from the roof is shaped like a feather: Jodorowsky reads an energy coming out, not a bolt striking."],
        ["The crown flying off", "The top opens: what is closed needs a way out."],
        ["Those who fall", "Two figures on the ground: falling is also touching the earth."],
        ["The spheres", "A rain of spheres: seeds, not rubble."]]},
{"es": "The Star", "kw": "trust, generosity, one's own place", "sombra": "naivety, scattering, utopia",
 "mirar": "The first naked figure. Kneeling by the water, she pours two jars: one into the stream and one onto the earth. Above, a great star and seven smaller ones; a bird perches in a tree. This is the action of seven in the second series: acting in the world by giving, finding one's own place.",
 "amp": "After the Tower comes calm: the hope left at the bottom of Pandora's box, the star of the Magi, Ishtar and Venus, the morning star. Nichols calls it a ray of hope. For Houdouin its old spelling, LESTOILLE, is proof of antiquity; for philology, a variant to be read with caution.",
 "q": ["What do you give without expecting anything in return?", "Where is your place in the world?", "Which star do you follow?"],
 "hs": [["Eight stars", "One large and seven small: eight lights, an ordered sky."],
        ["The jars", "She pours water onto the earth and into the river: giving to the flow and giving to the concrete."],
        ["The bird", "A witness in the tree: the soul that observes."],
        ["Naked and kneeling", "No mask, no clothes: finding one's own place in the world."]]},
{"es": "The Moon", "kw": "intuition, dream, receptivity", "sombra": "illusion, fear, confusion",
 "mirar": "The moon, in profile inside a disc, draws drops up from the earth. Below, two dogs howl between two towers, and a crayfish emerges from a pool. There is no human figure: it is the landscape of the psychic night. This is the receptive perfection of eight in the second series.",
 "amp": "Selene, Artemis and Hecate: the waxing, the full and the dark moon. The dogs recall Cerberus, guardian of the threshold; the crayfish climbs out of the primordial water, the oldest thing in us. Nichols asks whether she is maiden or menace: the moon illuminates and deceives with the same light.",
 "q": ["Which dream keeps coming back lately?", "What surfaces from the depths when you keep still?", "What frightens you about the unknown, and what draws you to it?"],
 "hs": [["The crayfish", "It climbs out of deep water: the archaic rising to the surface."],
        ["The two dogs", "Tame and wild at once, they howl at the same light."],
        ["The rising drops", "The moon attracts: receptivity has its own gravity."],
        ["The two towers", "Two thresholds on either side: between them, the unknown."]]},
{"es": "The Sun", "kw": "joy, clarity, new construction", "sombra": "dazzlement, self-centredness, dependence",
 "mirar": "A great sun with a face sheds coloured drops on two children, almost twins, who touch each other in front of a low wall. It is the crisis of nine seen from the light: not a solitary ending, like the Hermit's, but a shared regeneration. The wall protects what is beginning.",
 "amp": "Apollo, Helios, Ra; the Dioscuri, inseparable twins; the alchemical rubedo, the work in red and gold. Nichols calls it the radiant centre. For Jung, the sun symbolises the Self and a consciousness that warms without burning: wholeness recognising itself in the other.",
 "q": ["With whom do you share your light?", "What are you building anew?", "What part of you deserves to come out into the light?"],
 "hs": [["The twins", "One touches the other: joy as encounter, not as possession."],
        ["The low wall", "It protects what is new while it grows."],
        ["The drops", "The sun gives without choosing to whom."],
        ["The face of the sun", "A sun with a face: light as presence, not as phenomenon."]]},
{"es": "Judgement", "kw": "call, awakening, vocation", "sombra": "guilt, judgement, unfulfilled desire",
 "mirar": "An angel blows a trumpet from the clouds. Below, a figure seen from behind rises from a tomb between a man and a woman who pray. This is degree ten of the second series: what began to turn with the Wheel ends here with an irresistible call.",
 "amp": "The Last Judgement of Romanesque tympana, the resurrection of the dead, the phoenix. Nichols titles it a calling: the vocation that awakens. In Jungian language, the emergence of a new consciousness that integrates father and mother, past and future.",
 "q": ["Which call are you hearing and not yet answering?", "What part of you is coming out of the tomb?", "What did you inherit from your parents that you can now choose?"],
 "hs": [["The trumpet", "A sound that awakens: you do not choose it, you answer it."],
        ["The figure seen from behind", "We do not see its face: it could be anyone. It could be you."],
        ["Those who pray", "On either side, two figures witness the birth: the feminine and the masculine, mother and father."],
        ["The banner", "The angel carries a flag: awakening has its emblem."]]},
{"es": "The World", "kw": "fulfilment, wholeness, plenitude", "sombra": "stagnation, perfectionism, not knowing how to close",
 "mirar": "A figure dances inside a mandorla of leaves. In the four corners, an angel, an eagle, a lion and an ox. It is the last numbered card and the key to orientation: the four creatures are the four suits, the four elements and the four functions of the psyche around a centre.",
 "amp": "The tetramorph of Ezekiel and the Apocalypse, the four evangelists around Christ in majesty; the world soul; the mandala that Jung recognised in his patients' drawings as an image of the Self. Nichols calls it a window on eternity: the end of the journey, which is also a new beginning.",
 "q": ["What have you completed and not yet celebrated?", "Which corner of your world still needs to be integrated?", "If your life were a dance, which step would you be on?"],
 "hs": [["The mandorla", "An almond of light: life contained and protected."],
        ["The four creatures", "Here the lion and the ox appear; the angel and the eagle can be sensed above. Four elements, four suits, four functions."],
        ["The wand", "The Magician's tool is still here, but now it dances."],
        ["The ox", "The earth: even the heaviest part belongs to the whole."]]},
]

